No they're not!

NTSC television is 525 lines (625 for PAL).  About 400-475 of those are
actually usable on most real televisions.

There's a "color trap" at
3.58 Mhz, meaning that if you want all your pixels usable, dots can be
going onto the screen at 7Mhz or so at most (9mhz for B&W display).

The Apple ][ was *very* close to that; that's why the mad scheme for
color in high resolution graphics worked (it shifted the pixel by half a
pixel to jigger the trap).  It had 280 hroizontal pixels.  With smaller
margins on the left and right, you can exceed that by a bit, but not
all that much (and if you do, you'll spill off the edges of many
displays).

Of course, on some uncommon equipment, you can shoot clean past that
3.58mhz barrier, and thus use more pixels, but televisions that will
actually display that are rare (there's no reason for the manufacturer
to spend the extra to do so, as it has no benefit on real television
signals.)

